---
import SystemPage from '../../components/v2/SystemPage.astro';
import { imagery } from '../../content/imagery';

const v2 = imagery.v2;
---

<SystemPage
	title="Attic ventilation"
	heroEyebrow="Residential · service"
	heroHeading="Attic ventilation.<br />The reason roofs fail early."
	heroLede="Ridge, soffit, gable, and powered ventilation. The single biggest factor in residential roof longevity in East Texas — and the most overlooked. We balance intake and exhaust to manufacturer spec."
	heroPhoto={v2.pageHeros.atticVentilation}
	heroCta={{ label: 'Get my free inspection', href: '/free-inspection' }}
	bleedPhoto={v2.bleeds.workmanship}
	bleedHeading="Intake at the soffits.<br />Exhaust at the ridge."
	overview={{
		eyebrow: 'Why ventilation matters',
		heading: 'Heat and humidity kill roofs from the underside.',
		body: "An East Texas attic in July reaches 140–160°F if it's not properly ventilated. That heat cooks the underside of the deck, accelerates shingle aging, and shortens the roof's life by 5–10 years. Humidity adds rot and mold. The fix is balanced ventilation — intake at the soffit, exhaust at the ridge — sized to the attic. Most roofs we replace early traced back to a ventilation problem, not a roof problem."
	}}
	keyPoints={[
		{
			heading: 'Ridge + soffit (the right answer for most roofs).',
			body: 'Continuous ridge vent at the peak combined with continuous soffit vent at the eaves. Cool air enters at the soffits, rises through the attic, exits at the ridge. No moving parts, no electricity, works every minute the wind blows or the air column rises. Manufacturer-spec ratio: 1 sq ft net free area per 300 sq ft attic floor, split 50/50 intake/exhaust.',
			bullets: ['Continuous ridge vent + continuous soffit vent', '1:300 ratio (net free area : attic floor)', 'Split 50/50 intake/exhaust', 'No moving parts, no maintenance'],
			photo: v2.serviceTiles.attic,
			photoSide: 'left',
		},
		{
			heading: 'When powered or solar makes sense.',
			body: 'Powered (electric) and solar attic fans help on roofs where the ridge geometry prevents continuous ridge vent, or where the attic is unusually deep and natural convection isn\'t enough. They\'re a band-aid on roofs with bad soffit intake — adding exhaust without adding intake just sucks conditioned air out of your house. We diagnose first.',
			bullets: ['Solar attic fans (no electric draw)', 'Powered fans · last resort', 'Always pair with adequate soffit intake'],
			photo: v2.bleeds.workmanship,
			photoSide: 'right',
		},
	]}
	faq={[
		{ q: "What's the right balance?", a: '<p>Code is 1 sq ft of vent area per 300 sq ft of attic floor, split 50/50 intake and exhaust. Best practice in humid climates like East Texas is 1 per 150 (more aggressive). We measure your attic floor and spec accordingly.</p>' },
		{ q: "How do I know if my attic isn't ventilating?", a: '<p>Telltales: shingles aging unevenly (south face baked, north face fine), nail-pop, attic temperature in summer >140°F, frost on the underside of the deck in winter, mold on rafters, ice damming on the eaves in cold spells. Any one of these means the ventilation is short.</p>' },
		{ q: "Solar fan vs powered fan?", a: '<p>Solar if you\'re going to add forced exhaust at all — no electric draw, runs only when sun loads the roof (which is when you need it). Powered fans pull conditioned air from your house through any leaks in the ceiling, which often costs more in HVAC than the fan saves.</p>' },
		{ q: "Will fixing ventilation void my roof warranty?", a: '<p>No — proper ventilation is required by every major manufacturer\'s warranty (GAF, Owens Corning, CertainTeed, Atlas, Malarkey). Inadequate ventilation is what voids warranties. Fixing it brings the warranty back into compliance.</p>' },
	]}
	ctaHeading="Free inspection.<br />We measure your ventilation."
	ctaLede="If your roof is aging early, ventilation is the first place we look."
	ctaPhoto={v2.pageHeros.freeInspection}
	ctaPrimary={{ label: 'Get my free inspection', href: '/free-inspection' }}
/>
